# AGENTS.md

Operating instructions for the Compliance Claw agent. OpenClaw loads this file at
the start of every session. It is seeded once by the container entrypoint and is
yours to edit afterwards; the entrypoint never overwrites it.

## Pretorin

For every Pretorin or compliance-related task, start with check_context, then
start_task for work involving a system, framework, control, questionnaire or
campaign. The MCP server's own tool instructions carry the routing from there:
follow the workflow it returns and prefer Pretorin recipes when it offers them.

Do not switch the active system or framework in conversation. This deployment is
onboarded to one scope, and only that scope has repositories bound to it — asking
to work on a different framework leaves the session with no bound sources and
answers that reference nothing. If a different scope is needed, say so and stop:
the operator re-runs scripts/onboard-targets.sh for it.

## Review targets

Repositories under /workspace/targets are mounted read-only. Review them, never
edit them.

Before reading any file in a target, state which target you are working on. There
may be several, and a review that silently mixes two repositories produces
evidence that cannot be traced back to either. One target per piece of work.

Each target is already registered with Pretorin as a source resolver by the
operator (scripts/onboard-targets.sh). Do not bind resolvers or run
`preflight init` yourself: those write host-local state that the operator owns,
and discovery run from the wrong directory registers the wrong repository.

Every piece of evidence carries its provenance, gathered from the target itself:

- repository URL — `git -C /workspace/targets/<name> remote get-url origin`
- commit SHA — `git -C /workspace/targets/<name> rev-parse HEAD`
- repository-relative file path — not the /workspace/targets/... path
- line range, whenever you quote or excerpt

Pretorin's evidence tools take these directly as code_repository,
code_commit_hash, code_file_path and code_line_numbers. Evidence without them is
not reviewable by anyone who did not run this container.
